+	/* Acknowledge BEFORE reading the channel status, and keep going until the vector is quiet.
+	 *
+	 * The clear used to happen at the end of the handler, after every loop status had been read. Any
+	 * descriptor that completed in between had its notification destroyed by that clear: the status
+	 * read had already missed it, and the bit it set was wiped on the way out. The count then stays
+	 * one short of the batch with no further interrupt coming to correct it.
+	 *
+	 * In loop mode this is invisible, because interrupts keep arriving and the next one recomputes the
+	 * count from a free running register. In one-shot mode the last interrupt of a batch is the only
+	 * one that will ever be sent, so losing it hangs the transfer for good.
+	 *
+	 * Clearing first shrinks that window but does not close it: anything arriving while the channels
+	 * are being serviced is still only visible to whatever this pass happened to read. So the pass
+	 * repeats until the vector comes back empty, and a notification landing at any point during
+	 * servicing is picked up by the next iteration instead of being lost. At worst the loop runs one
+	 * extra time and finds nothing to do.
+	 *
+	 * The repeat is bounded, and the bound is what keeps this a hard IRQ handler rather than a hang. A
+	 * card that has left the bus reads as all ones, every register of it, so its vector never comes
+	 * back empty and an open loop would hold this CPU for good. All ones is recognised and ends the
+	 * handler at once, and any other vector that will not clear ends it after LITEPCIE_IRQ_PASSES
+	 * passes. A notification still pending then is not lost to the host: the transfer it belongs to
+	 * runs into the caller's deadline and is redone.
+	 *
+	 * Multi-vector MSI and MSI-X have no shared status register to re-read, since the vector is implied
+	 * by the irq number. There a single pass is all there is, so the loop runs exactly once.
+	 */
+#ifndef CSR_PCIE_MSI_CLEAR_ADDR
